# Operational Automation

- Fail on errors and return clear exit codes with structured, redacted output.
- Validate resolved targets before writes, moves, deletes, remote actions, or migrations.
- Quote literal paths and avoid string-built destructive commands.
- Make repeated execution safe and idempotent; provide validation/dry-run mode where feasible.
- Never print secrets or pass them on command lines where process inspection can expose them.
- Python services use supported runtimes, locked environments, typed public interfaces, schemas, and structured logs.
- Bound network, subprocess, queue, GPU, upload, and file work with timeouts and size limits.
- Workers require durable state, idempotency, cancellation, restart recovery, and observable failure.
- Unsafe deserialization, dynamic execution, and shell interpolation are prohibited.
